    <statement><p>Let
<me>y = 2x^{4}\mathopen{}\left(x^{7}-9\right)</me>
Calculate the derivative using the product rule.</p>
<p><m>f(x) =</m>  <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0001"/> and <m>g(x)=</m> <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p>which means that</p>
<p><m>f'(x) =</m>  <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0003"/> and <m>g'(x)=</m> <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0004"/></p>
<p>And thus <m>y' =</m> <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0005"/> <m>+</m>  <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0006"/></p>
<p>(It doesn't matter which part of the sum you enter first or second.)</p>
<p>Try checking your answer by multiplying <m>y</m> out and then using power rule.</p>
</statement>
    <hint><p><m>y</m> is a product of two functions, <m>2x^{4}</m> and <m>x^{7}-9</m>.</p>
<p>The product rule says that <m>\left[f(x)g(x)\right]'=f'(x)g(x)+g'(x)f(x)</m>.</p>
</hint>
