      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>The function <m>y=2x^{4}\mathopen{}\left(x^{7}-9\right)</m> is a product of two functions, say <m>f(x)= 2x^{4}</m> and <m>g(x)=x^{7}-9</m>.  Then taking the derivatives we get <m>f'(x)=8x^{3}</m> and <m>g'(x)=7x^{6}</m>.  Finally, the product rule says that 
<me>\left[f(x)g(x)\right]'=f'(x)g(x)+g'(x)f(x)</me>
so <m>y'= (8x^{3}) \cdot (x^{7}-9) + (7x^{6})\cdot (2x^{4})</m>.</p>
</solution>
